// Koru Standard Library: Supervision
// ===================================
//
// Failure-handling as a compile-time fact, in the `stored`/`captured`
// family: the arm declares that its producing call is supervised, the
// transform elaborates the declaration into an ordinary bounded retry
// loop — the language's own `#`/`@` fold. There is no supervisor object,
// no registry, no runtime service: the policy is a datablock read at
// compile time and the mechanism is a generated step tor plus a labeled
// fold loop.
//
//   worker(n)
//   | ok v |> …
//   | boom f |> std/supervisor:supervised
//       | retry t when t < 3
//
// or, with an explicit re-entry call and terminal produce:
//
//   | boom f |> std/supervisor:supervised
//       | retry t when t < 3 |> worker(n: f + 4)
//       | exhausted => boom f
//
// The datablock `{ restart: N [, args: { f: expr }] }` is the terse spelling
// of the same guarantee — `restart: N` is `| retry t when t < N`, and
// `args:` is the implicit re-entry call's argument map. Under
// `[with]std/supervisor` the qualifier drops, but the bare name collides
// with produce-branch spelling — write `supervised()` there.
//
// SEMANTICS (pinned by 320_151's hand-written twin):
//   - `restart: N` means N retries after the initial call — the producing
//     call runs at most N+1 times.
//   - Re-entry targets the producing call; unmapped arguments keep the
//     LAST attempt's values (the step's ambient inputs), not the site's.
//   - Every non-supervised branch proceeds exactly as written at the site:
//     the sibling arms dispatch on the generated step's vocabulary, so a
//     retried `ok` flows through the same arm set as a first-call `ok`.
//   - Exhaustion forwards the LAST child outcome upward in kind — a
//     `| err` stays `| err`, a `?!boom` stays a panic. The supervisor
//     absorbs and defers; it never rewrites an outcome's kind.
//   - The retry counter rides as a step input named after the retry arm's
//     own binding (`| retry t` → field `t`) — `when` conditions and
//     re-entry args resolve verbatim, no shadowing, no `__fail` plumbing.
//
// ELABORATION — the language's own `#`/`@` fold, no generated proc:
//
//   connect = dial(port)                     tor __sup_step_L<n> { port, t }
//   | ok => ok                               | ok | refused
//   | refused |> supervised                  | __more { port, t }
//       | retry t when t < 5
//         |> dial(port: port + 1)       ==>  __sup_step_L<n> = dial(port)
//       | exhausted => refused               | ok => ok
//                                            | refused when t < 5
//                                              => __more { port: port+1, t: t+1 }
//                                            | refused => refused
//
//                                            connect = #__sup_L<n> __sup_step_L<n>(port, t: 0)
//                                            | __more s |> @__sup_L<n>(s.port, s.t)
//                                            | ok => ok
//                                            | refused => refused
//
// The step's `port` IS the failing attempt's argument — ambient reader
// semantics, not threading — so `port + 1` reads the failed port. Branch
// shapes pass through verbatim: a void branch stays void inside the fold,
// so `| ok p` on a `| ok` declaration refuses at the site exactly as it
// would without `supervised`.

const std = @import("std");
~part h
~part decl
~part policy
~part fold
~part home
~part explain
